# Handoff Rules

## Sending Handoffs
- Write a draft handoff file with only structured headers, then run `swarm_handoff.sh <draft-file>`.
- Use only these message types:
  - `git_handoff`
  - `note`
- Do not send `note` handoffs unless the user, role prompt, or constitution
  explicitly directs you to send one.
- When blocked by ambiguity, contradiction, or test/specification conflict,
  stop and ask for clarification; do not send a `note` handoff unless one of
  the explicit authorities above directed that note.
- For `git_handoff`, commit first, then write:

```text
type: git_handoff
to: <role>[,<role>...]
priority: NN
task: <short-stable-task-name>
commit: <10-character-commit-abbrev>
```

- The reviewer adds one more required header to every `git_handoff`: `verdict: pass`
  or `verdict: fail`. No other role may send `verdict`.

## Routing Table
The swarm is a cycle. Each role has a fixed set of allowed recipients:

| Sender    | Allowed recipients                          |
| --------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| specifier | coder                                       |
| coder     | reviewer                                    |
| reviewer  | coder, architect                            |
| architect | coder, specifier                            |

- Every role may send work to the coder. The coder answers only to the reviewer.
- The reviewer's route follows its verdict: `verdict: pass` goes to the architect
  only (never directly to the specifier, however small the change and including
  re-reviews after rework); `verdict: fail` goes to the coder only. When the
  plan's review round cap is reached, a failing review is escalated to the
  architect only, and the swarm rejects it if addressed to the coder.
- Before every handoff to the coder, the specifier syncs with the remote:
  `git fetch origin --prune`, then `git pull --ff-only` on the base branch, and
  uses `git rev-parse --short=10 HEAD` as `commit:`. The swarm rejects a commit
  that is behind the base branch. If the pull cannot fast-forward, the
  specifier stops and tells the user instead of sending a stale commit.
- The reviewer and the architect each forward a git_handoff to exactly one role (a note may go to several recipients within your row).
- A handoff reaching the specifier from the architect means the feature is
  complete. The swarm publishes it as a pull request for the user to review
  and dispatches the next feature after the user merges it; the specifier only
  informs the user and handles failure notes.
- Never send to a recipient outside your row. A misrouted handoff stalls the
  cycle because the specifier never receives its completion signal.
- Always forward after completing an inbound task, regardless of what changed.
  Formatting-only, manifest-only, audit-only, generated metadata, and other
  non-functional churn still require a forward along the cycle.
- Preserve the received task name when forwarding work for the same task. If the
  handoff starts new work, invent a short stable task name.
- For `note`, write:

```text
type: note
to: <role>[,<role>...]
priority: NN
message: <one line, max 80 chars>
```

- If `swarm_handoff.sh` reports validation errors, repair the draft and rerun it.
- After a successful send, the helper removes the draft file. If you need to
  remove a stale draft manually, use `rm <draft-file>`, not `rm -f`.
- Do not write long handoff bodies. The helper generates the delivered payload.
- Do not send herdr pane notifications directly.
- Do not hand-edit, merge, stage, or commit handoff runtime state.

## Receiving Handoffs
- When notified, run `ready_for_next.sh`.
- If you are woken or told to check your inbox, only run `ready_for_next.sh`;
  never start another agent or role.
- `ready_for_next.sh` dispatches to the task or batch helper configured for
  your role.
- If it prints `NO_TASK`, stop waiting for work.
- If it prints `TASK: <path>`, treat the printed `PAYLOAD` as the task.
- If it prints `TASK_NAME: <name>`, use that as the stable task name for any
  work you forward from that task.
- If it prints `BATCH: <path>`, treat each printed `BATCH_ITEM` as part of the
  current batch in helper-delivered order.
- Use only the task information printed by the helper scripts.
- If a pane wake-up arrives while already working on a task, ignore it.
- When the task or batch is fully complete, run `done_with_current.sh`.
- The coder, reviewer and architect must send their own `git_handoff` for the
  task BEFORE running `done_with_current.sh`; it refuses (`REPLY_REQUIRED`)
  otherwise. Only if a step legitimately ends without a reply, run
  `done_with_current.sh --no-reply`.
- `note` handoffs are tasks too; after reading or acting on a note, run
  `done_with_current.sh` before accepting any other handoff.
- If `done_with_current.sh` prints `TASK: <path>`, treat the printed `PAYLOAD`
  as the next task.
- If `done_with_current.sh` prints `BATCH: <path>`, treat each printed
  `BATCH_ITEM` as part of the next batch in helper-delivered order.
- If a done helper prints `NO_TASK`, stop waiting for work.
- On restart, run `ready_for_next.sh` and follow its output.
